00:00:12.120 --> 00:00:16.040
fans of static typing will be pleased to&nbsp;
know about the biggest change in Python 3.12

00:00:16.040 --> 00:00:19.720
the new syntax for type&nbsp;
parameters and the type statement

00:00:19.720 --> 00:00:21.440
when defining a generic function in Python

00:00:21.440 --> 00:00:24.360
you can now declare type variables&nbsp;
for the function in brackets

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:27.520
previously you had to import TypeVar

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:29.840
and create your own type variable

00:00:30.440 --> 00:00:33.360
making sure the name that you assign&nbsp;
it to matches the name in quotes

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.720
that's no longer necessary just put brackets T

00:00:37.720 --> 00:00:40.920
you could also put multiple&nbsp;
type variables like this

00:00:40.920 --> 00:00:42.440
TypeVarTuples like this

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:44.440
and ParamSpecs like this

00:00:44.440 --> 00:00:48.920
you could also constrain the&nbsp;
allowed types like this or like this

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:53.120
this syntax is even more convenient&nbsp;
for defining generic classes

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:57.240
previously defining generic&nbsp;
classes was very confusing

00:00:57.240 --> 00:00:59.920
you have to import stuff and&nbsp;
define your own type variables

00:01:00.600 --> 00:01:02.080
and then you have to learn about category&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:05.480
theory and pick the right type of&nbsp;
variance for your type variable

00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:11.400
that used to be super annoying and confusing but&nbsp;
thanks to python 3.12 it's now no longer necessary

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:13.480
you don't even have to know&nbsp;
about covariance anymore

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:17.120
python just does the right thing&nbsp;
it automatically determines it

00:01:17.120 --> 00:01:21.920
you can now also use the type keyword&nbsp;
to define your own type aliases

00:01:21.920 --> 00:01:28.200
you could sort of already do this by declaring a&nbsp;
type alias like this but this usage is deprecated

00:01:28.200 --> 00:01:32.920
and notice that this type alias is&nbsp;
defined before Expected is defined

00:01:32.920 --> 00:01:36.920
that's fine these type&nbsp;
aliases are evaluated lazily

00:01:36.920 --> 00:01:40.000
and they can even be generic too go wild

00:01:40.000 --> 00:01:42.400
this new syntax is going to&nbsp;
make generics in Python a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.400 --> 00:01:47.240
less cumbersome to write so if you love&nbsp;
