00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:51.680
if you hate static typing&nbsp;
I'll still rate it 3 out of 10

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:57.760
next up PEP 701 f-strings are now officially&nbsp;
formally part of the Python grammar

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:01.960
for historical reasons they used to&nbsp;
be implemented in a kind of hacky way

00:02:01.960 --> 00:02:05.280
one of the consequences of that hack is that&nbsp;
you couldn't use the same kind of quotes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:10.160
started the f-string inside one of the&nbsp;
f-string expressions-- without escaping

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.840
python would see that quote and think you're&nbsp;
trying to end the f-string in an unbalanced way

00:02:14.840 --> 00:02:16.760
well no more you can now put whatever expression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.760 --> 00:02:19.680
you want inside an f-string&nbsp;
including another f-string

00:02:19.680 --> 00:02:24.080
but of course just because you can doesn't&nbsp;
mean you should-- keep things readable

00:02:24.080 --> 00:02:29.400
all around this is a great update that gets&nbsp;
rid of an ugly wart that python had 9 out of 10

00:02:29.400 --> 00:02:33.880
next up PEP 684 a unique per interpreter GIL

00:02:33.880 --> 00:02:36.360
did you know you can have&nbsp;
multiple python interpreters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.360 --> 00:02:39.040
in the same process-- even in the same thread

00:02:39.040 --> 00:02:40.960
here an interpreter means the part of python that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:45.320
runs in a loop reading and&nbsp;
executing python bytecode

00:02:45.320 --> 00:02:48.480
the main purpose of a sub&nbsp;
interpreter is to provide isolation

00:02:48.480 --> 00:02:51.120
each sub interpreter is like a&nbsp;
little sandbox that can execute&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:51.120 --> 00:02:53.400
code independently from the other interpreters

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:56.560
it has its own globals, imports, etc.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:02:59.200
an example usage is something like mod_wsgi

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.560
for security reasons a web server serving multiple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:05.240
different applications should keep&nbsp;
data for each application separate

00:03:05.240 --> 00:03:08.960
therefore mod_wsgi creates a sub&nbsp;
interpreter for each application group

00:03:08.960 --> 00:03:10.600
when a request comes in a worker will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.600 --> 00:03:14.080
acquire and use the sub interpreter&nbsp;
dedicated to that application group

00:03:14.080 --> 00:03:17.880
so where does PEP 648 come in? well&nbsp;
python has this thing called the GIL

00:03:17.880 --> 00:03:19.440
the global interpreter lock

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:22.640
but that also means python is limited&nbsp;
in multi-threaded performance because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.640 --> 00:03:26.080
only the thread that currently holds&nbsp;
the GIL can actually run python code

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:28.200
while sub interpreters do provide isolation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:30.920
there's still a small amount of&nbsp;
state that's shared between them

00:03:30.920 --> 00:03:34.960
and in particular the GIL is--&nbsp;
or WAS shared between them

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:36.800
this PEP makes it so each interpreter gets&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.240
its own GIL which will allow True&nbsp;
multi-threaded performance in Python

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:45.320
for the very small number of projects&nbsp;
that actually use sub interpreters

00:03:45.320 --> 00:03:47.640
in summary a per interpreter GIL is a great idea&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.640 --> 00:03:51.640
it just only applies to a&nbsp;
few projects so 6 out of 10

00:03:51.640 --> 00:03:55.720
have you ever tried debugging your python&nbsp;
application and found it to be unbearably slow

00:03:55.720 --> 00:03:58.440
compared to when you just run it normally

00:03:58.440 --> 00:04:02.040
introducing pep 669 low impact monitoring

00:04:02.040 --> 00:04:04.200
well here's a direct quote from the pep

00:04:04.200 --> 00:04:06.520
by using quickening we expect that code run under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.520 --> 00:04:12.520
a debugger on 3.12 should outperform&nbsp;
code run without a debugger on 3.11

00:04:12.520 --> 00:04:16.400
previously python debuggers worked using&nbsp;
sys.settrace which allows essentially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.400 --> 00:04:19.360
the same functionality in a less efficient way

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:23.880
the new sys.monitoring namespace provides&nbsp;
a clean API to register for events

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.840
and the implementation details allow&nbsp;
it to take advantage of the ongoing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:29.600
work of specializing instructions at runtime

00:04:29.600 --> 00:04:32.960
"quickening" as it was mentioned&nbsp;
before is the process of replacing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:37.120
slow instructions with faster&nbsp;
specialized versions at runtime

00:04:37.120 --> 00:04:40.600
this is all part of the faster&nbsp;
CPython project link below

00:04:40.600 --> 00:04:44.760
anyway better debugging I'm&nbsp;
definitely in 8 out of 10

00:04:44.760 --> 00:04:49.120
next up better error messages&nbsp;
particularly for common typos

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:53.760
did you forget a self dot somewhere&nbsp;
or use the wrong syntax for an import

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:56.760
now python will tell you&nbsp;
exactly what you did wrong

00:04:56.760 --> 00:04:59.080
these are small changes but&nbsp;
literally every developer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:02.880
benefits from better error messages 10 out of 10

00:05:02.880 --> 00:05:08.520
ahh yes PEP 683 Immortal&nbsp;
objects-- this is a juicy one

00:05:08.520 --> 00:05:12.240
do you run a Django app with over&nbsp;
2 billion monthly active users

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:15.920
if so this is the most exciting&nbsp;
python 3.12 update for you

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:19.320
you see if you have two billion users&nbsp;
you're going to need to fork a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.320 --> 00:05:22.680
worker processes to handle all&nbsp;
those requests they generate

00:05:22.680 --> 00:05:26.400
good thing forking is so fast on&nbsp;
Linux because it uses copy on write

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.960
but wouldn't it just be a darn shame if&nbsp;
every read in Python was also a write

00:05:30.960 --> 00:05:34.560
oh wait every read in Python is&nbsp;
also a write because when you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.560 --> 00:05:38.200
read a python object first&nbsp;
you increase its ref count

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:40.240
that's a write

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.640
too bad that's going to trash&nbsp;
your cache and ruin your day

00:05:43.640 --> 00:05:46.520
introducing Immortal objects

00:05:46.520 --> 00:05:50.960
twiddle one of those high bits in an&nbsp;
object's ref count and poof it's now Immortal

00:05:50.960 --> 00:05:54.000
Immortal objects do not participate&nbsp;
in ref counting because they're meant&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.000 --> 00:05:57.240
to live forever-- at least&nbsp;
until interpreter shut down

00:05:57.240 --> 00:06:00.840
there's no point in keeping track of&nbsp;
how many people have references to None,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.720
True, False, and other global immutable built-ins

00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:05.520
make them Immortal too

00:06:05.520 --> 00:06:09.320
with Immortals now you can have&nbsp;
a read that's really just a read

00:06:09.320 --> 00:06:13.200
2 out of 10 I don't have 2&nbsp;
billion monthly active users

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:16.840
next up PEP 709 comprehension inlining

00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:19.440
previously dictionary list&nbsp;
and set comprehensions like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.440 --> 00:06:23.080
these were defined under the hood using functions

00:06:23.080 --> 00:06:27.840
meaning it literally compiles the inside&nbsp;
of a comprehension as a separate function

00:06:27.840 --> 00:06:30.640
which it then instantiates and calls immediately

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:33.280
that creates a bit of overhead&nbsp;
because you now have to create&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.280 --> 00:06:37.120
this function object and create a&nbsp;
stack frame when you call the function

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:39.240
well that's no more they&nbsp;
change the implementation,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.240 --> 00:06:43.040
dictionary list and set comprehensions&nbsp;
no longer use functions under the hood

00:06:43.040 --> 00:06:46.280
all the comprehensions get compiled&nbsp;
inline inside this function

00:06:46.280 --> 00:06:48.280
hopefully you won't really notice this change

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:51.680
you'll get a slightly different traceback if&nbsp;
you have an exception inside a comprehension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.680 --> 00:06:55.480
because there's no longer this stack&nbsp;
frame associated with the comprehension

00:06:55.480 --> 00:06:58.040
and since comprehensions are now&nbsp;
compiled inline you can now see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:58.040 --> 00:07:00.840
the local variables of the outer&nbsp;
function with a call to locals

00:07:00.840 --> 00:07:05.240
as opposed to the local variables&nbsp;
of that inner lambda function scope

00:07:05.240 --> 00:07:08.680
I mean it's a tiny Improvement&nbsp;
but why not 6 out of 10

00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:12.800
next up PEP 688 using the&nbsp;
buffer protocol from python

00:07:12.800 --> 00:07:16.280
the buffer protocol is a common interface&nbsp;
allowing many libraries like numpy to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:16.280 --> 00:07:19.120
interoperate as long as they can&nbsp;
conceptualize their data storage

00:07:19.120 --> 00:07:21.440
as some kind of n-dimensional array determined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.000
by a few properties like the number of&nbsp;
dimensions shape strides and element size

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.320
previously the buffer interface&nbsp;
was only accessible in the C API

00:07:30.080 --> 00:07:33.280
this pep exposes that interface&nbsp;
to python by adding two new magic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.280 --> 00:07:36.520
dunder methods __buffer__ and __release_buffer__

00:07:36.520 --> 00:07:39.840
the usefulness of buffers in Python&nbsp;
is limited but it could be useful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:44.560
for debugging or potentially building a&nbsp;
bridge type that connects two C extensions

00:07:44.560 --> 00:07:47.720
I probably won't use it 4 out of 10

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:51.760
next up PEP 692 using TypedDict to annotate kwargs

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:56.600
the default way that you annotate keyword&nbsp;
arguments in Python is not very intuitive

00:07:56.600 --> 00:07:59.200
kwargs is always going to be&nbsp;
a dictionary and the keys of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:01.840
the the dictionary are always going to be strings

00:08:01.840 --> 00:08:05.920
so originally python decided that that means&nbsp;
you just need to annotate the value type

00:08:05.920 --> 00:08:11.560
so if I annotate kwargs as an int this means&nbsp;
that it's a dictionary of string to int

00:08:11.560 --> 00:08:14.680
that's already confusing enough&nbsp;
but also when would I want just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:19.320
an arbitrary number of extra keyword&nbsp;
arguments that all have to be integers?

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:23.040
if you have a use case I'd love to&nbsp;
hear your thoughts comment below

00:08:23.040 --> 00:08:24.440
if I was going to take a bunch of keyword&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.440 --> 00:08:27.760
arguments I'd probably want them&nbsp;
to have some specific structure

00:08:27.760 --> 00:08:30.520
a typed dictionary seems like a&nbsp;
perfect match for defining the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.520 --> 00:08:33.640
structure of a dictionary so why don't we use that

00:08:33.640 --> 00:08:36.520
we can't just type hint kwargs&nbsp;
as a movie because remember&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:36.520 --> 00:08:40.200
that's going to be interpreted as&nbsp;
a dictionary of strings to movies

00:08:40.200 --> 00:08:43.400
well in 3.12 they added Unpack&nbsp;
into the typing library which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.400 --> 00:08:46.680
instructs the type checker to do&nbsp;
what you would hope it would do

00:08:46.680 --> 00:08:50.760
namely treat kwargs like it's supposed&nbsp;
to be one of these typed dictionaries

00:08:50.760 --> 00:08:54.560
I'll give it 4 out of 10 I guess I would&nbsp;
really prefer to do this instead of using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.560 --> 00:08:59.200
kwargs but kwargs are part of the language so&nbsp;
we should at least be able to type them properly

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:04.160
next up developers who are paid by the&nbsp;
line of code are going to love this one

00:09:04.160 --> 00:09:08.600
PEP 698 the typing override decorator

00:09:08.600 --> 00:09:10.360
let's say you have a class hierarchy

00:09:10.360 --> 00:09:14.040
we have a base class shape&nbsp;
that has a calc_area method

00:09:14.040 --> 00:09:17.360
then we have squares and&nbsp;
triangles that are both shapes

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:21.480
both Square and Triangle have their own&nbsp;
specialized way of calculating their area

00:09:21.480 --> 00:09:25.800
the programmer then uses the override&nbsp;
decorator to signal that yes this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:30.680
implementation of calc_area is meant to be&nbsp;
an override of something in a base class

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:35.320
this doesn't really do much at runtime but&nbsp;
when we run our type checker we find a problem

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:40.560
the method used to be called get_area but we&nbsp;
did a big refactor and it's now called calc_area

00:09:40.560 --> 00:09:44.160
but it looks like we missed a spot and&nbsp;
there's still a get_area floating around

00:09:44.160 --> 00:09:47.120
the base class may have even&nbsp;
had a correct albeit slower&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:47.120 --> 00:09:53.040
implementation of calc_area for the triangle&nbsp;
so potentially all of our tests still pass

00:09:53.040 --> 00:09:56.880
but because this method is marked override&nbsp;
and there's no get_area function left in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.880 --> 00:10:01.800
parent class this now becomes a type error&nbsp;
that we can catch at static analysis time

00:10:01.800 --> 00:10:05.120
there's also a suggested strict mode&nbsp;
that would make it a type error if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.120 --> 00:10:09.880
didn't mark the function as override when&nbsp;
it was overriding something in a base class

00:10:09.880 --> 00:10:12.520
this could potentially stop you from&nbsp;
accidentally overriding something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.520 --> 00:10:15.920
in a base class that you didn't&nbsp;
know existed in the first place

00:10:15.920 --> 00:10:20.720
my only fear is this might be the second&nbsp;
step along the way to becoming C++ with their

00:10:20.720 --> 00:10:26.160
"public static constexpr auto const override"&nbsp;
that you need to slap on every definition

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:29.160
hopefully it won't come to that 5 out of 10

00:10:29.160 --> 00:10:33.760
next up PEP 689 introducing&nbsp;
the Unstable C API tier

00:10:33.760 --> 00:10:36.840
lots about the C API in this release, huh?

00:10:36.840 --> 00:10:42.160
this pep introduces the unstable API which has the&nbsp;
same compatibility requirements as the public API

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:45.960
but does not require deprecation&nbsp;
warnings before change

00:10:45.960 --> 00:10:50.800
so this contains things that are basically&nbsp;
expected to change with every version of python

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:52.840
this would be useful for something like a debugger&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.840 --> 00:10:57.840
that might use specific op codes that are&nbsp;
only valid in specific versions of python

00:10:57.840 --> 00:11:02.000
currently everything the unstable&nbsp;
tier is related to code objects

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:08.200
so unless you're using the C API this probably&nbsp;
won't affect you but good to be aware 6 out of 10

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:12.640
and the most painful change not&nbsp;
even a pep for this one setuptools&nbsp;&nbsp;

